3.1.3 Acting on several bookmarks at once
-----------------------------------------

The commands labelled _selection-scoped_ above act on every row marked
with ‘m’.  With no row marked they act on the bookmark at point, so the
single-bookmark case needs no marking.

   Mark a few rows with ‘m’, then ‘t a’ adds the tags you type to all of
them, ‘t r’ removes from all of them, and ‘t e’ replaces the tag list on
all of them.  The prompts report how many bookmarks are involved.  For
‘t e’ and ‘t r’ the tags offered are the union across the marked rows: a
tag on any one of them is available, and removing a tag that a given
record does not carry leaves that record unchanged.

   The two toggles set the whole selection to a single value rather than
flipping each row independently: ‘T’ and ‘^’ turn the property *on* for
every marked row unless all of them already have it, in which case they
turn it *off* for every marked row.  A selection with the property on
some rows and off on others therefore becomes uniform on the first
press, and both target states remain reachable by pressing again.  This
does not depend on where point sits — point need not be on a marked row
at all.

   Completing one of these commands clears the selection, so the next
command starts from a clean slate rather than inheriting a set of marks
you have stopped thinking about.  Ending a tag reader without entering
anything changes nothing and leaves the marks in place, so an accidental
‘RET’ does not cost you the selection.

   The deletion flag ‘D’ (set with ‘d’) is a separate mark, and ‘x’
remains the only command that acts on it.  Flagging rows with ‘d’ does
not make them a selection for ‘t a’, ‘T’, or the other selection-scoped
commands, and deselecting after a completed command leaves the ‘D’ flags
untouched.

   ‘x’ obeys the same view bounding: it deletes the flagged rows the
buffer currently shows, counts only those in its confirmation prompt,
and leaves hidden flagged rows — and their flags — alone.  With every
flagged row hidden it refuses, the same way the selection-scoped
commands do.  Narrow the view, and what ‘x’ can delete is exactly what
you can see.

   A selection is bounded by what the buffer displays.  Rows hidden by
an active filter or by ‘H’ are never acted on, even if they were marked
before being hidden, and their marks are left alone: lift the filter and
they are still marked, ready for a second command.  So narrowing with
‘/’ after marking is a way to apply a command to part of a selection and
keep the rest for later.

   This bounding covers everything a command derives from the selection,
not just which records it writes to — the union of tags offered by ‘t e’
and ‘t r’, and the on/off value a toggle settles on, are computed from
the visible rows alone.

   When the filter hides every marked row, the command fails immediately
with ‘No selected bookmarks in this view’, before prompting for
anything.  It does not fall back to the bookmark at point: you asked for
the selection, and acting on the row at point instead would modify a
bookmark you did not select, while reporting success over an empty
selection would suggest the marked bookmarks had been dealt with.  The
marks are left intact — widen the filter and run the command again.

   The fallback to the bookmark at point applies only when nothing is
marked at all.

   ‘r’ (rename), ‘R’ (relocate), ‘a’ (annotate), ‘i’ (inspect), and the
jump commands remain row-scoped: each acts on the bookmark at point and
ignores marks, since each needs a value that is specific to one record.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Persisting view state,  Prev: Acting on several bookmarks at once,  Up: The *Bookmarks-gt List* buffer

3.1.4 Persisting view state
---------------------------

The list buffer's sort column, ‘show-temp’ toggle, and active filters
are preserved across repeated invocations of ‘bookmark-gt-list’ within a
session.

   To also carry the view across Emacs sessions, set
‘bookmark-gt-list-persist-state’ to a non-nil value.  When enabled, the
three values are written to ‘bookmark-gt-list-state-file’ (default:
‘(locate-user-emacs-file "bookmark-gt-list-state")’) on every
state-changing command and on ‘kill-emacs-hook’, and re-read on the next
'bookmark-gt-list-mode' entry.

     (setq bookmark-gt-list-persist-state t)

   Selection marks and point are never persisted.  Use ‘V’ or ‘M-x
bookmark-gt-list-reset-view’ to restore defaults; the reset itself is
persisted when the file is enabled.


