3.3.3 Function bookmarks — ‘bookmark-gt-create-function’
--------------------------------------------------------

A function bookmark stores a callable (a symbol or lambda) under the
‘function’ alist key.  Jumping invokes the callable.

   Interactive:

     M-x bookmark-gt-create-function RET
     Function bookmark name: rebuild-project RET
     Function to call on jump: my-project-rebuild RET

   Elisp:

     (bookmark-gt-create-function "rebuild" #'my-project-rebuild)
     (bookmark-gt-create-function "flip-theme"
                               (lambda () (my-toggle-theme)))


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Keyboard-macro bookmarks — bookmark-gt-create-kmacro,  Next: Sequence bookmarks — bookmark-gt-create-sequence,  Prev: Function bookmarks — bookmark-gt-create-function,  Up: Non-file bookmark types

3.3.4 Keyboard-macro bookmarks — ‘bookmark-gt-create-kmacro’
------------------------------------------------------------

A kmacro bookmark stores a key-event vector under the ‘kmacro’ alist key
and replays it on jump via ‘execute-kbd-macro’ (with
‘current-prefix-arg’ as the repeat count).

   Interactive: prompts for the macro via a single completion whose
candidates are the current named keyboard macros plus a
‘[last-kbd-macro]’ sentinel that stands for the anonymous most-recent
macro.  The sentinel is the default when ‘last-kbd-macro’ is defined;
otherwise the first named macro is the default.  Errors when neither
exists.

   Named keyboard macros are any symbols whose ‘symbol-function’ is a
string or vector, or which carry a ‘kmacro’ property (as produced by
‘M-x kmacro-name-last-macro’).

   Elisp:

     (bookmark-gt-create-kmacro "type-hi" [?h ?i])
     (bookmark-gt-create-kmacro "type-abc" "abc")   ; parsed by `read-kbd-macro'

   Unlike bookmark+, which overloads the function-bookmark handler (its
‘bmkp-jump-function’ dispatches by payload type), bookmark-gt gives
kmacros their own type and handler so the list buffer, narrow, and
filter can distinguish them.  Trade-off: kmacro records are NOT
interchangeable on disk with bookmark+ (a bookmark+ macro record still
classifies as Function under bookmark-gt because the record's handler
symbol is ‘bmkp-jump-function’).


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Sequence bookmarks — bookmark-gt-create-sequence,  Next: File-type handlers — override the file-jump path per pattern,  Prev: Keyboard-macro bookmarks — bookmark-gt-create-kmacro,  Up: Non-file bookmark types

3.3.5 Sequence bookmarks — ‘bookmark-gt-create-sequence’
--------------------------------------------------------

A sequence bookmark stores a list of bookmark names under the ‘sequence’
alist key.  Jumping visits each in order via ‘bookmark-jump’.

   Interactive: prompts for the sequence name, then reads existing
bookmark names one at a time until you enter empty input.

   Elisp:

     (bookmark-gt-create-sequence "morning-workspace"
                               '("inbox" "todo.org" "calendar"))


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: File-type handlers — override the file-jump path per pattern,  Next: View bookmarks — save the list buffer's current view,  Prev: Sequence bookmarks — bookmark-gt-create-sequence,  Up: Non-file bookmark types

3.3.6 File-type handlers — override the file-jump path per pattern
------------------------------------------------------------------

‘bookmark-gt-file-type-handlers’ is an alist mapping a filename regexp
to a bookmark handler function.  When jumping a file bookmark whose
‘filename’ matches, the mapped function is called with the bookmark
record instead of the built-in ‘find-file’ path.  The function's
contract is the same as any 'bookmark.el' handler: one argument, the
record.

   Typical use: open certain file types with an external program instead
of an Emacs buffer.

     (defun my-mac-open (bookmark)
       "Open BOOKMARK's file with macOS's `open' command."
       (call-process "open" nil 0 nil
                     (bookmark-prop-get bookmark 'filename)))

     (setq bookmark-gt-file-type-handlers
           '(("\\.pdf\\'"          . my-mac-open)
             ("\\.\\(mp4\\|mkv\\)\\'" . my-mac-open)))

   Entries are tried in list order; the first matching regexp wins.  The
advice on ‘bookmark-default-handler’ is installed by ‘bookmark-gt-mode’
and removed when the mode turns off.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: View bookmarks — save the list buffer's current view,  Prev: File-type handlers — override the file-jump path per pattern,  Up: Non-file bookmark types

3.3.7 View bookmarks — save the list buffer's current view
----------------------------------------------------------

A view bookmark captures the presentation state of the ‘*Bookmarks-gt
List*’ buffer — active filters, sort key, and the show-temp toggle —
under the ‘filters’, ‘sort-key’, and ‘show-temp’ alist keys.  Jumping
opens the list buffer (if needed), restores the state, and redraws.

   To save: open the list, set the filters/sort/show-temp you want, then
‘M-x bookmark-gt-create’.  The list mode installs its own
‘bookmark-make-record-function’, so the standard set command captures a
view record automatically — no dedicated command is needed.  Marks and
point are intentionally not saved; they are per-session, not part of the
view.

   Elisp: construct the record via ‘bookmark-gt-create-non-file’ with
handler ‘bookmark-gt-handler-view-jump’ and the three props; see
‘bookmark-gt-list--make-record’ for the shape.


