4.1 Tag reader
==============

‘bookmark-gt-tags-read’ prompts for tags one at a time.  Each prompt is
a plain ‘completing-read’ against every tag that currently appears in
‘bookmark-alist’, ordered most-recently-used first (based on
‘bookmark-gt-tags-history’; completion history is the MRU signal).

   Terminators:
   • Empty ‘RET’ — finish, return the accumulated list.
   • ‘M-RET’ — submit the current input (if non-empty), then finish.

   Any non-empty ‘RET’ accepts the current tag and prompts for another.
The running accumulator is shown in the prompt as ~Tags [foo,bar] (M-RET
or empty RET to finish): ~.

   The reader is invoked by ‘bookmark-gt-create’ when
‘bookmark-gt-prompt-for-tags-flag’ is non-nil (default) and seeds itself
with any tags provided by ‘bookmark-gt-default-tags-mode’ or another
‘bookmark-gt-create-tag-reader-hook’ contributor.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Bookmarks that share a name,  Next: Changing an existing bookmark,  Prev: Tag reader,  Up: Setting bookmarks

4.2 Bookmarks that share a name
===============================

A bookmark name is a label, not an identifier.  Two bookmarks may share
one — a ‘todo’ in each of several projects is the case this exists for.
What they cannot share is a name *and* a destination: that pair is
almost always a second bookmark created by accident, in a place that
already has one.

   ‘bookmark-gt-allow-same-name-bookmarks’ decides when
‘bookmark-gt-create’ may reuse a name:

Value                               Creating with a name already in use
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘never’                             refused
‘different-destination’ (default)   allowed when the two point at different files or URLs; refused for the same one
‘always’                            allowed

   A refusal is an error naming the conflict.  The bookmark is not
stored under an altered name: appending ‘<2>’ would make names unique
behind your back, which is exactly what allowing shared names exists to
avoid.  When you meant to move the existing bookmark rather than add
one, the error says so — use ‘bookmark-gt-update’.

   Destination means the target, not the position.  Two bookmarks in one
file at different points share a destination.

* Menu:

* Telling them apart on screen::
* Loading a file that breaks the setting::
* If you expected two records and see one::
* Which command reaches which record::
* Record identity::


