4.2.1 Telling them apart on screen
----------------------------------

Records sharing a name are displayed with a ‘<N>’ suffix — ‘todo’,
‘todo<2>’ — the convention Emacs uses for buffers it cannot otherwise
distinguish.

   The suffix is computed for display and never stored.  The name in the
bookmark file is the one you typed, so deleting or renaming one of the
records renumbers the rest rather than leaving a stale suffix behind,
and nothing you did not type ends up in your bookmark file.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Loading a file that breaks the setting,  Next: If you expected two records and see one,  Prev: Telling them apart on screen,  Up: Bookmarks that share a name

4.2.2 Loading a file that breaks the setting
--------------------------------------------

‘bookmark-gt-create’ cannot produce a name the setting forbids, but a
bookmark file can: written by the built-in bookmark package, by
bookmark-gt under a different setting, or on another machine.

   When such a file is loaded, the records the setting forbids are
omitted from ‘bookmark-alist’ and a warning lists them.  The first
record of a name is always kept; under the default a later one is kept
when it points somewhere no kept record does.

   *The omission is in memory only.*  Those bookmarks are still in the
file, and stay there until it is next saved — at which point they are
gone from it too.  To keep them, set the option to ‘always’ and reload
before saving.

   Omitting them is not counted as a change, so it never schedules a
save on its own.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: If you expected two records and see one,  Next: Which command reaches which record,  Prev: Loading a file that breaks the setting,  Up: Bookmarks that share a name

4.2.3 If you expected two records and see one
---------------------------------------------

A record can be stored and still be absent from the list buffer, so
there are two possible causes.  Count the stored records to determine
which one applies:

     (seq-filter (lambda (r)
                   (equal (substring-no-properties (car r)) "todo"))
                 bookmark-alist)

   *Two records.*  Both were stored; the list buffer is not displaying
one of them.  Either:

   • *A filter is active.*  Filters on tags, group, or type restrict
     which records ‘bookmark-gt-list’ prints; ‘bookmark-alist’ is
     unmodified.  Filters persist across sessions via
     ‘bookmark-gt-list-state-file’, so a filter set in an earlier
     session is still in effect.  ‘V’ resets the view to the defaults;
     ‘/’ sets a filter.
   • *One record is temporary and temporary records are hidden.*
     Temporary records are omitted from the display, not from
     ‘bookmark-alist’, and they remain reachable by ‘bookmark-gt-jump’.
     ‘H’ toggles their visibility.  (‘T’ changes whether the record at
     point is temporary, which modifies the record — it is not a display
     toggle.)

   *One record.*  Only one was stored under that name.  Either
‘bookmark-gt-create’ refused the second, because
‘bookmark-gt-allow-same-name-bookmarks’ forbade it — the error says so
at the time — or it was omitted when the bookmark file was loaded, which
produces a warning listing the records left out.  See Loading a file
that breaks the setting.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Which command reaches which record,  Next: Record identity,  Prev: If you expected two records and see one,  Up: Bookmarks that share a name

4.2.4 Which command reaches which record
----------------------------------------

‘bookmark-get-bookmark’ resolves a name with ‘assoc’ and returns the
*first* record carrying it.  Anything built on that reaches only one of
a set of namesakes, and which one depends on the order records sit in
‘bookmark-alist’.

   *bookmark-gt's commands resolve a reference properly.*  Given a
record they act on it; given a name shared by several bookmarks they ask
which one rather than choosing.  The jump reader and the list buffer
work from records throughout, so selecting the second of two same-named
bookmarks jumps to, renames, relocates or deletes that one.  Jumping
also increments the visit count on the record you jumped to, so
most-recently-used ordering stays right when names repeat.

   *The built-in commands are remapped while ‘bookmark-gt-mode’ is on*,
so ‘M-x bookmark-delete’, ‘bookmark-rename’, ‘bookmark-jump’ and
‘bookmark-set’ reach bookmark-gt's versions and inherit that.  Turning
the mode off restores the built-ins, which act on the first match —
Emacs's own ‘bookmark-delete’ docstring says it "removes only the first
instance of a bookmark with that name".

   *Only the commands are remapped.*  The functions are unchanged,
because packages call them from Lisp and depend on what they do:
‘org-capture’ and ‘org-refile’ store onto a fixed name on every capture
and rely on ‘bookmark-set’ overwriting by name.  Such a name is a
singleton by construction, so first-match resolution is right for it.

   From Lisp, ‘bookmark-gt-jump-record’, ‘bookmark-gt-rename-record’ and
‘bookmark-gt-delete-record’ take a record and signal if given a name, so
a caller holding a record cannot act on a different one.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Record identity,  Prev: Which command reaches which record,  Up: Bookmarks that share a name

4.2.5 Record identity
---------------------

Each record carries a ‘bookmark-gt-id’ — a symbol assigned when the
bookmark is created, and to records that lack one when the bookmark file
is loaded, the list buffer redraws, or the jump reader opens.  It is
what lets a reference survive a rename: a sequence bookmark stores its
members by id rather than by name.

   The id is internal.  Nothing asks you to type one, and it appears
only in ‘i’ (describe record) in the list buffer and in the bookmark
file itself.  The built-in bookmark package ignores the key, so a file
bookmark-gt has written still loads there.

   Records written by other packages have no id until bookmark-gt next
reads them, and a file copied between machines can hold the same id
twice.  Both cases are expected: a bookmark is resolved by id when it
has one and by name otherwise.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Changing an existing bookmark,  Next: Deleting,  Prev: Bookmarks that share a name,  Up: Setting bookmarks

4.3 Changing an existing bookmark
=================================

Three commands change a bookmark rather than create one.  Which to reach
for depends on what you are changing and where you are standing.

Command                  Changes                               You are
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘bookmark-gt-update’     where it points, to here              at the new place
‘bookmark-gt-relocate’   where it points, to a path you type   anywhere
‘bookmark-gt-rename’     its name                              anywhere

   Each takes a bookmark record, a name, or an id, and prompts when
given none.  Given a name shared by several bookmarks they ask which one
rather than picking the first.

* Menu:

* bookmark-gt-update — point it at where I am::
* bookmark-gt-relocate — point it somewhere I name::
* bookmark-gt-rename::


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: bookmark-gt-update — point it at where I am,  Next: bookmark-gt-relocate — point it somewhere I name,  Up: Changing an existing bookmark

4.3.1 ‘bookmark-gt-update’ — point it at where I am
---------------------------------------------------

Re-captures the location and puts it on the existing record.  The
bookmark keeps its name, its id, its tags, its annotation, its creation
time and its visit history; everything describing where it points is
replaced.  Replacing all of it is what removes a previous type's
leftovers — a PDF record's page data, an org record's heading id, a
Dired record's marks.

   It asks before changing a bookmark's type, since turning a PDF
bookmark into an org one is a large change to something you already
have.

   It signals for bookmarks with no buffer location — URL, function,
kmacro and sequence — because there is nothing at point to capture for
them.  Use ‘bookmark-gt-relocate’ for those.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: bookmark-gt-relocate — point it somewhere I name,  Next: bookmark-gt-rename,  Prev: bookmark-gt-update — point it at where I am,  Up: Changing an existing bookmark

4.3.2 ‘bookmark-gt-relocate’ — point it somewhere I name
--------------------------------------------------------

Reads the new target instead of capturing it, so it works without
visiting anything, and it is the only way to change a bookmark that has
no buffer location:

   • File records prompt with ‘read-file-name’, defaulting to the
     current filename, so fixing a moved file is a matter of editing the
     path.  ‘position’ is left alone — the usual reason to relocate is a
     file that moved, and the offset is still good.
   • URL records — including browser-tab bookmarks — prompt for the new
     URL.
   • Records with neither raise a ‘user-error’.

   It refuses a target of a different type: pointing a PDF bookmark at
an org file would leave a handler that cannot open what it now points
at.  Changing type is what ‘bookmark-gt-update’ is for.

   In the list buffer, ‘R’ relocates the row's record.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: bookmark-gt-rename,  Prev: bookmark-gt-relocate — point it somewhere I name,  Up: Changing an existing bookmark

4.3.3 ‘bookmark-gt-rename’
--------------------------

Renames the bookmark given, not the first one sharing its name.  The new
name is checked the same way a new bookmark's is, so renaming cannot
produce something creating could not — see Bookmarks that share a name.

   In the list buffer, ‘r’ renames the row's record.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Deleting,  Prev: Changing an existing bookmark,  Up: Setting bookmarks

4.4 Deleting
============

‘M-x bookmark-gt-delete’ deletes the bookmark given rather than the
first record sharing its name.  Before deleting it reports any sequence
bookmark that referenced it, since that reference is about to stop
resolving and that is worth knowing now rather than at the next jump.

   In the list buffer, ‘d’ flags a row and ‘x’ deletes what is flagged.


